---
// /mesonet/ag_tools/feels_like/: feels-like temperature, at the old path.
// Source: mco-website/_pages/mesonet/tools/mesonet_tools_feels_like.md.
// Text and coefficients unchanged; equations are linear HTML (T*T → T²).
// The example call moved from the legacy API, framed in an iframe, to a
// plain link on mesonet2 (same station, dates and element; answered 200 on
// 23 Oct 2026).
import Base from '../../../layouts/Base.astro';
import { url } from '../../../lib/paths';
import { DASHBOARD } from '../../../lib/tools';
import { API_BASE } from '../../../lib/mesonet';

const WIND_CHILL = 'https://www.weather.gov/media/epz/wxcalc/windChill.pdf';
const HEAT_INDEX = 'https://www.wpc.ncep.noaa.gov/html/heatindex_equation.shtml';
const EXAMPLE = `${API_BASE}/derived/daily/?stations=crowagen&start_time=2023-12-01&end_time=2023-12-15&elements=feels_like&type=csv`;
---
<Base short="Feels-like temperature"
      description="How the Montana Mesonet computes the feels-like temperature from wind chill, heat index and air temperature.">
  <!-- Copy drafted with AI assistance; needs MCO staff review before launch (UM AI disclosure). -->
  <div class="prose">
    <h1>Feels-like temperature</h1>
    <p>
      The "Feels Like" temperature is a combination of the heat index, wind chill, and air
      temperature.
    </p>

    <h2>Wind chill</h2>
    <p>
      Wind chill combines the air temperature and wind speed to provide a real feel temperature
      when temperatures are less than 50 °F and wind speed is greater than 3 mph
      (<a href={WIND_CHILL}>National Weather Service wind chill equation</a>):
    </p>
    <p class="eq">
      Wind chill (°F) = 35.74 + 0.6215 <var>T</var> − 35.75 <var>V</var><sup>0.16</sup>
      + 0.4275 <var>T</var> <var>V</var><sup>0.16</sup>
    </p>
    <p>where <var>T</var> is the temperature in °F and <var>V</var> is wind speed in miles per hour.</p>

    <h2>Heat index</h2>
    <p>
      When temperatures are above 80 °F, the heat index is used to calculate the feels like
      temperature. The heat index combines temperature and relative humidity to provide a real feel
      temperature in warm conditions (<a href={HEAT_INDEX}>NOAA Weather Prediction Center heat
      index equation</a>):
    </p>
    <p class="eq">
      <var>HI</var> = −42.379 + 2.04901523 <var>T</var> + 10.14333127 <var>RH</var>
      − 0.22475541 <var>T</var> <var>RH</var> − 0.00683783 <var>T</var><sup>2</sup>
      − 0.05481717 <var>RH</var><sup>2</sup> + 0.00122874 <var>T</var><sup>2</sup> <var>RH</var>
      + 0.00085282 <var>T</var> <var>RH</var><sup>2</sup>
      − 0.00000199 <var>T</var><sup>2</sup> <var>RH</var><sup>2</sup>
    </p>
    <p>
      where <var>T</var> is the temperature in °F and <var>RH</var> is the relative humidity
      expressed as percent.
    </p>
    <p>
      If <var>RH</var> is less than 13% and the temperature is between 80 and 112 °F, the following
      is subtracted from the <var>HI</var> value given above:
    </p>
    <p class="eq">
      ((13 − <var>RH</var>) / 4) × √((17 − |<var>T</var> − 95|) / 17)
    </p>
    <p>
      If <var>RH</var> is greater than 85% and the temperature is between 80 and 87 °F, the
      following is added to the <var>HI</var> value given above:
    </p>
    <p class="eq">
      ((<var>RH</var> − 85) / 10) × ((87 − <var>T</var>) / 5)
    </p>

    <h2>Air temperature</h2>
    <p>
      If the temperature, wind speed, or relative humidity don't meet the criteria for calculating
      the wind chill or heat index, raw temperature is used to calculate the feels like temperature.
    </p>

    <h2>Get the data</h2>
    <p>
      The Mesonet API serves the feels-like temperature as the <code>feels_like</code> element. For
      example, <a href={EXAMPLE}>daily feels-like temperature at Crow Agency E (<code>crowagen</code>), 1 to 15 December
      2023, as CSV</a>.
    </p>

    <p class="method-note">
      Part of the <a href={url('mesonet/ag_tools/')}>agricultural tools</a>.
      The <a href={`${DASHBOARD}#charts`}>Station Dashboard</a> charts it for each station under
      Charts, Ag tools.
    </p>
  </div>
</Base>
